///// 6.09


Split the Admin Web API from the app API.

The session you get now depends on which login you called, not on whether the account
is an admin. This is what fixes admins being logged out of the app on the admin schedule.

New: "/admin/auth/login"  -> admins only, admin session, expires after 1 day
     "/admin/auth/request-login", "/admin/auth/logout", "/admin/auth/logout-all", "/admin/auth/me"

Unchanged for the app: "/auth/login" -> everyone, admin or not, gets 365 days.
Nothing to change in the app. The "sessionType" field still works but is no longer needed.

Every admin route also answers under "/admin" now: the new path is "/admin" + the old one.
Old paths still work until the panel has moved. Exceptions that do NOT move:
  "POST /reports"                  student bug submission, app API
  "GET /data/lectures", "GET /data/professor", and the single-item reads   public

LoginResponse gained a field:
public record LoginResponse(
        String message,
        boolean success,
        String authToken,
+++     String expiresAt
) {}


///// 8.09


Admin sessions are one day again.

The two-minute admin session was a temporary experiment, to watch that a short admin
lifetime could no longer log an administrator out of the app. It could not, and the
experiment is over: "POST /admin/auth/login" issues a one-day session from the next
deployment on.

Nothing about the app API changed. "POST /auth/login" still returns 365 days, for
administrators too. The lifetime is decided by the endpoint you called, not by the account.

Read "expiresAt" from the login response rather than assuming either number.


///// 8.09 (2)


Creating a lecture or a professor is now recorded in the audit log.

Two new values can appear in "GET /admin/audit-logs" and in its "/meta" enum list:

    LECTURE_CREATED
    PROFESSOR_CREATED

Both are administrative actions. Updating or deleting a lecture already wrote an entry;
creating one wrote nothing, so the log could show a rename with no record of where the
row came from.

Neither is revertible, and that is deliberate: the entry is a lifecycle event
("changes": {"exists": {"before": false, "after": true}}), not a field diff, so
"POST /admin/audit-logs/{id}/revert" answers ACTION_NOT_REVERTIBLE. Undoing a creation
is a deletion, which is its own audited action. This is the same shape
USER_WARNING_CREATED has always had.

Panels that render the action list from "/meta" pick these up on their own. One that
hard-codes the vocabulary needs the two values added.

Also: three fields in the audit responses are now declared nullable in the OpenAPI
schema, because they always could be null and the schema said otherwise --
"revertBlockedReason" and "revertedByAuditId" on an entry, and "target.id", which a
refusal has no value for. No response changed; only the schema now describes them
correctly.


///// backfill -- shipped between 20.08 and 8.09, never written down here


Everything below this line was already released and was missing from this file. It is
collected here rather than back-dated into the middle, so nothing that was written at the
time appears to have changed. It comes out of the four test layers; docs/test-findings.md
holds the full reasoning for each, under the finding number given in brackets.


1. Failures carry HTTP status codes now, not 200.  [F-5]

Was: 200 {"message": "Error, couldn't find student", "success": false}
Now: 404 {"message": "Error, couldn't find student", "success": false}

StudentService, AccountService and SocialService served every failure as a 200 with
"success": false, while the moderation services already threw and answered 400/404 for the
same situations. Two error contracts in one API. They are one now.

The BODY IS UNCHANGED in every case. A client that reads "success" has nothing to do. A
client that reads the status now sees the failure it was already being told about.


2. A vote can be taken back.  [F-3]

VoteType gained a third value:

    UP
    DOWN
+++ NONE     withdraws the vote -- the row is deleted

Send "NONE" to POST /social/comments/vote/comment/{id} or .../vote/answer/{id} to remove a
vote. Withdrawing a vote that was never cast is not an error.

Additive on purpose: a client that only ever sends UP or DOWN cannot tell the difference. A
second identical vote still rewrites the first, exactly as before -- making that a toggle
would have changed silently what an existing client's retry does.


3. An invalid "voteType" is a 400 instead of a 409.  [F-2]

Was: 409 {"message": "State conflict"}          (from a database constraint, at flush time)
Now: 400 {"message": "Invalid request"}

A null or unknown voteType used to travel down to the column and fail there. It is rejected
at the boundary now, and the status says "your request was wrong" rather than "the server is
in a conflicting state".


4. The answer-vote route had a missing slash.  [F-4]

Was: POST /social/comments/vote/answer{answer_id}     -> /social/comments/vote/answer<uuid>
Now: POST /social/comments/vote/answer/{answer_id}

BREAKING, and deliberately not served on both paths: the old spelling was a typo, not a
contract. An Android build still on the old path gets a 404 on the vote call. The comment
side (.../vote/comment/{comment_id}) was always correct and is untouched.


5. A wrong HTTP verb is a 405, not a 404.  [F-16]     -- every route

Was: 404 {"message": "Not found"}
Now: 405 {"message": "Method not allowed"}  + an "Allow" header naming the verbs that work

A path that does not exist is still a 404. Until now the two were indistinguishable from the
response, which is why "POST /admin/reports" looked like a missing route rather than a route
that only answers GET.


6. A body in the wrong media type is a 415, not a 500.  [F-17]   -- every route with a body

Was: 500 {"message": "Unexpected backend error"}
Now: 415 {"message": "Unsupported media type"}

Malformed JSON inside the CORRECT media type was and stays a 400. So: 415 means "do not send
me text/plain", 400 means "your JSON is broken".


7. Two routes that answered 500 answer properly now.  [F-14, F-15]

    GET /data/professor/id            without firstName/lastName    500 -> 400
    GET /ratings/{lectureId}/categories   unknown lecture id        500 -> 404


8. GET /ratings/own/{lectureId} no longer answers "ratings": null.

Was: {"message": "...", "success": false, "ratings": null}
Now: {"message": "...", "success": false, "ratings": []}

null was the answer for any lecture the student had not rated -- which is every lecture that
has no ratings yet. A client iterating the field without a null check crashed on it. It is
an empty list now, always.

An unknown lecture id on the same route is a 404 rather than a successful-looking body, the
same answer GET /ratings/{lectureId}/categories gives beside it.


9. GET /system/status reports whether location lookup is working.  [F-8]

+++ "locationLookupEnabled": true | false

Next to "gitlabEnabled" and for the same reason. False means IPINFO_TOKEN is missing or
revoked and login mails will say "Unknown" for the location -- which used to happen with no
log line at all.


10. Three message typos are corrected.  [F-10]

Was: "Error, couldnt authenticate user"    Now: "Error, couldn't authenticate user"
Was: "Error, couldnt find student"         Now: "Error, couldn't find student"
Was: "Deleted Account succesfully: ..."    Now: "Deleted Account successfully: ..."

They reach the user interface through BasicResponse.message. If you match on these strings,
match on the corrected spelling.


Not in this file, because no interface changed: the four test layers (unit, API/role,
contract, integration, end-to-end -- 867 tests), the CI/CD repairs, and the audit and revert
fixes that are internal. docs/test-plan.md and docs/test-findings.md hold those.


///// 8.09 (3)


LectureResponse gained two fields, and its professor list has a fixed order now.

public record LectureResponse(
        UUID id,
        String name,
        String code,
        int semesterYear,
        SemesterSeason semesterSeason,
+++     String semesterLabel,
+++     String title,
        boolean active,
        LectureType lectureType,
        List<ProfessorShortResponse> professors,
        int commentCount,
        int ratingCount,
        double averageRating
) {}

"semesterLabel" is the semester the way it is written at the university:

    SS26        summer semester 2026
    WS25/26     winter semester, which spans two calendar years

"semesterYear" on a winter lecture is the year the semester STARTS in. Say so if your data
means the other one, because nothing in the schema records which.

"title" is what a rating screen puts above a lecture:

    "SS26 Algorithmen 1 — Peter Sanders, Anna Bauer"

and, for a lecture with nobody assigned to it, just "SS26 Algorithmen 1" — no trailing dash.
Both fields are derived: there is no column behind either, and neither can be written.

They are computed here and not in each client so that the "WS25/26" rule lives in one place.
Three apps formatting it themselves is three chances to publish "WS25".

"professors" is now ordered by last name, then first name, then id, in every response that
carries a lecture. It used to come out of a Set in whatever order the JVM felt like, which
could differ between two requests for the same lecture. If you build your own title, this is
what made it reshuffle. The same applies to "lectureIds" in ProfessorResponse, ordered by
lecture name now.

One thing this does NOT do: the order is alphabetical, not "lecturer first, exercise leaders
after". The database records that a person teaches a lecture and nothing more, so nobody can
tell a professor from an Übungsleiter. That needs a new column on lecture_professors — tell
us if you want it.

Also corrected in docs/admin-api.md, where it had been wrong since the file was written: the
LectureResponse example showed "semesterSeason":"WINTER". The only accepted values are "SS"
and "WS"; "WINTER" is answered with 400 {"message":"Invalid request"}.


///// 8.09 (4)


"GET /auth/me" is gone for good. Use "GET /admin/auth/me".

It had already stopped answering some weeks ago -- it was removed from the app API and the
removal was deliberate, but this file and docs/admin-api.md still described it as a legacy
path that would keep working until the panel had migrated. They no longer do. There is no
plan to bring it back.

    GET /auth/me          removed, answers 404
    GET /admin/auth/me    unchanged, and the one to call

The admin route returns exactly what the old one did:

  {"message", "success", "user": {"id", "username", "kitEmail", "role", "status",
                                  "isSuperAdmin"}}

Panel teams: this was the fifth line of task 1 in docs/adminweb-tasks.md. It is not a
migration any more, it is a deletion -- if anything still calls "/auth/me" it is already
getting a 404.


///// 8.09 (5)


The four create failures carry a status code now. F-5, finished.

"POST /data/lectures" and its "/admin" twin:

Was: 200 {"message": "Lecture already exists: <name>", "success": false}
Now: 409 {"message": "Lecture already exists: <name>", "success": false}

Was: 200 {"message": "Professor not found: <uuid>", "success": false}
Now: 404 {"message": "Professor not found: <uuid>", "success": false}

"POST /data/professor" and its "/admin" twin:

Was: 200 {"message": "Professor already exists: <first> <last>", "success": false}
Now: 409 {"message": "Professor already exists: <first> <last>", "success": false}

Was: 200 {"message": "Couldn't find lecture with lectureID: <uuid>", "success": false}
Now: 404 {"message": "Couldn't find lecture with lectureID: <uuid>", "success": false}

The BODY IS UNCHANGED in all four, message text included. A client that reads "success" has
nothing to do. A client that reads the status -- or that treats a non-2xx as a transport
error and never opens the body -- now has to handle 409 and 404 on these two routes.

These were the last two services answering a create failure with 200. The rest moved under
F-5 in the backfill above; these two were left behind because four tests pinned the old
answers. Those tests were inverted rather than deleted, so they state the new contract now.

Nothing else on either route changed. A successful create still answers 200 with
"success": true, and an invalid body is still 400 {"message": "Invalid request"}.


///// 9.09


The two catalogue detail reads answer 404 for a row that is not there.

"GET /data/lectures/{id}":

Was: 200 {"message": "Couldn find lecture with uuid: <uuid>", "success": false, "lecture": null}
Now: 404 {"message": "Lecture not found", "success": false}

"GET /data/professor/{id}":

Was: 200 {"message": "Couldn't find prof with uuid: <uuid>", "success": false, "professor": null}
Now: 404 {"message": "Professor not found", "success": false}

Note the SECOND line of each, not only the status. The error body is the project's standard
{"message", "success"}, so the "lecture" / "professor" key is now ABSENT rather than null. A
client reading `body.lecture == null` is unaffected -- a missing key deserialises to null in
Jackson, Moshi and Gson alike. A client that checks whether the key is present has to change.
The message text changed too, so anything matching on it has to change.

These were the last two reads answering a failure with 200. Every other read in the catalogue
-- "GET /ratings/{lectureId}", "GET /ratings/{lectureId}/categories" -- has answered 404 since
F-5 and F-15. Both routes are public, so an unknown id was reachable without a token.

A successful read is unchanged: 200 with "success": true and the payload.


///// 9.09 (2)


"POST /audit-logs/{id}/revert" no longer answers 500 for a refusal entry.

Was: 500 {"message": "Unexpected backend error", "success": false}
Now: 409 {"message": "LOGIN_REFUSED cannot be reversed", "success": false,
          "reason": "ACTION_NOT_REVERTIBLE"}

Applies to LOGIN_REFUSED and ACCESS_REFUSED -- the entries that record something that did NOT
happen, and therefore carry no target id. Both reach the panel through "GET /activity-logs",
so an operator could see one and click revert on it.

Panel teams: this is the answer "GET /activity-logs" was already giving you for these rows.
Its "revertBlockedReason" said ACTION_NOT_REVERTIBLE and the revert behind it crashed, so a
panel that trusted the list never noticed and one that let the operator try got a 500 with
nothing to show them. Nothing to change if you read "revertible" from the list; if you
special-cased the 500, that can go.

Every other revert answer is unchanged.


///// 9.09 (3)


The four "/social" submit routes answer 400 for a bad id instead of 500.

    POST /social/comments          "lectureID"
    POST /social/answers           "commentID"
    POST /social/comments/report   "commentID"
    POST /social/answers/report    "answerID"

Was: 500 {"message": "Unexpected backend error", "success": false}
Now: 400 {"message": "Invalid lecture id", "success": false}          (malformed)
Now: 400 {"message": "Invalid request", "success": false}             (missing)

Two cases, and the second is the one worth reading twice: an id that is MISSING was a 500 as
well, not only a malformed one. The routes now also require "content" on the two submits and
"reportReason" on the two reports, which were unvalidated and could reach the database as
null. "explanation" stays optional.

Was: 500 for {"lectureID":"not-a-uuid","content":"x"}
Was: 500 for {"content":"x"}                            -- no lectureID at all
Was: 500 for {"lectureID":"<valid>"}                     -- no content
Now: 400 for all three.

Nothing changes for a well-formed request. If you were treating a 500 from these routes as
"retry later", that retry was never going to work -- the request was the problem, and it now
says so.


///// 9.09 (4)


Two more malformed bodies that answered 500 now answer 400.

"POST /ratings/rate" -- "lectureId" and "topics" were both unvalidated:

Was: 500 for {"topics":[]}                                  -- no lectureId
Was: 500 for {"lectureId":"<uuid>"}                         -- no topics
Was: 409 for a topic with no "category"                     -- reached the column as null
Now: 400 {"message": "Invalid request", "success": false}   -- all three

"topics" is required but may still be EMPTY: an empty list is answered exactly as before, so
only a body that omits the field entirely changes. A topic's "value" is still unconstrained --
nothing in the backend defines the scale, so nothing here invents one.

"POST /data/lectures" and "POST /admin/data/lectures" -- "professorIds" omitted:

Was: 500 {"message": "Unexpected backend error", "success": false}
Now: 200, the lecture is created with no professors

This one is the opposite direction on purpose. The field is optional -- the assignment is
writable afterwards through "PATCH /admin/data/professor/{id}" -- and "POST /data/professor"
has always read its mirror field that way. Sending "professorIds": [] was already accepted and
is unchanged; only omitting the key was broken.


///// 9.09 (5)


"ratingCount" on a professor is the number of ratings. It used to be 0, always.

    GET /data/professor
    GET /data/professor/{id}
    GET /admin/data/professor/all

Was: "ratingCount": 0      for every professor, however many ratings they had
Now: "ratingCount": 12     the ratings on every lecture they teach

The field was read from a stored column that nothing in the application ever wrote -- no
default, no trigger, no code path. "averageRating" beside it has been recomputed on read all
along and was correct, which is why this was not obvious from the response.

Panel and app: if you were hiding or ignoring this field because it was always 0, it is worth
showing now. If you were displaying it, it was wrong and now is not.


///// 9.09 (6)


"GET /ratings/{lectureId}" returns its categories in a fixed order.

Was: "ratings" in an unspecified order that changed between restarts, and differed between
     two instances of the service answering the same request
Now: "ratings" in RatingCategory declaration order -- ROOM, ORGANIZATION, MATERIALS,
     WORKLOAD, LECTURE_*, PROFESSOR_*, EXERCISE_*, TUTOR_*

The averages were collected in a HashMap keyed by the enum, and an enum's hash code is its
identity hash, so the order was whatever the JVM's object layout produced that run. The route
is public and unauthenticated.

Nothing to change if you sort or look up by category. If you were rendering the list in the
order it arrived, it will stop reshuffling -- this is the same defect F-21 fixed for a
lecture's professor list.


///// 9.09 (7)


"POST /ratings/rate" answers 404 for a lecture that does not exist.

Was: 200 {"message": "Error, cannot find lecture with id <uuid>", "success": false}
Now: 404 {"message": "Lecture not found", "success": false}

The last route in the ratings module still reporting a failure with 200. The two reads either
side of it -- "GET /ratings/{lectureId}" and "GET /ratings/{lectureId}/categories" -- have
answered 404 for the same unknown id since F-15, so a client had to handle both conventions
depending on which of the three it called.

The message text changed, so anything matching on it has to change. A successful submission is
untouched.


///// 9.09 (8)


"POST /admin/audit-logs/{id}/revert" now works on a report status change that hid content.

Was: 409 {"message": "COMMENT_REPORT_STATUS_CHANGED cannot be reversed",
          "success": false, "reason": "ACTION_NOT_REVERTIBLE"}
Now: 200 -- the report goes back to its previous status and the comment or answer it hid
     becomes visible again

The listing said so too: "revertible" was false on those entries and "revertBlockedReason"
read ACTION_NOT_REVERTIBLE, so a panel drawing the button from that flag correctly did not
draw it. Both now report revertible: true.

Only the entries that changed visibility were affected -- that is, every transition into or
out of ACTION_TAKEN, which is the whole reason the revert exists for this action. Those
entries record two changed fields ("status" and "commentStatus"/"answerStatus") and the
revert handler published only the first, so the service refused the entry as carrying a
field it could not restore.

Panel: entries you were hiding the revert button on will now offer it. Nothing to change if
you read "revertible" from the response, which is what it is there for.


///// 9.09 (9)


"POST /admin/data/lectures" stores the name and code trimmed, and refuses a duplicate that
differs only by whitespace.

Was: "  Algorithmen 1  " stored with its padding, and accepted alongside an existing
     "Algorithmen 1"
Now: stored as "Algorithmen 1", and the second one answers
     409 {"message": "Lecture already exists: Algorithmen 1", "success": false}

"POST /admin/data/professor" already stored the trimmed name but ran its uniqueness check on
the untrimmed one, so " Stefan" / "Kuehnlein " walked past the 409 and created a second
professor holding exactly the name the first one holds. It now checks what it stores.

Was: 200, a duplicate professor row
Now: 409 {"message": "Professor already exists: Stefan Kuehnlein", "success": false}

Nothing to change if you already trim in the form. If you were relying on padding to create
near-duplicate rows, that no longer works -- which was the point.

One side effect worth knowing: a lecture created with a padded name used to record a bogus
"name" change in the audit log on its first admin edit, because the PATCH path trims before
comparing and the stored value did not. New lectures cannot get into that state.


///// 9.09 (10)


"PATCH /admin/users/{id}" refuses to demote an administrator who has dispositioned a bug
report.

Was: 200, the administrator was demoted and the bug reports it had dealt with lost their
     attribution silently
Now: 409 {"message": "Administrator has moderation history and cannot be demoted",
          "success": false}

No new rule -- this is the rule that was already documented, applied to the third of its three
sources. Demotion has always been refused for an administrator who issued a warning or
reviewed a comment or answer report. Bug reports were meant to count too and could not:
"resolved_by_admin_id" existed with a foreign key and nothing in the application had ever
written it.

"PATCH /admin/reports/{id}" now records who changed a bug report's status, and when, the same
way the comment and answer report endpoints have always recorded it. Neither field appears in
any response, so the only thing a client sees is the 409 above. Moving a report back to OPEN
clears them again.

Panel: an administrator who only ever worked the bug queue can no longer be demoted. Strip the
attribution first, or leave the account an administrator -- the same choice that already
applied to warnings.


///// 9.09 (11)


Four more lists that had no defined order now have one. Same defect as F-21 and F-27, in the
places those two fixes did not reach.

    GET /ratings/own/{lectureId}
    GET /admin/ratings

Was: "ratings" / "topics" in whatever order the database returned them
Now: RatingCategory declaration order -- OVERALL, ROOM, ORGANIZATION, MATERIALS, WORKLOAD,
     LECTURE_*, PROFESSOR_*, EXERCISE_*, TUTOR_*

The same order "GET /ratings/{lectureId}" has had since 9.09 (6). One response field cannot
have two orders, and these two were reading a JPA collection with no ORDER BY while the third
had been given an EnumMap.

    GET /social/comments/{lecture_id}
    GET /social/sync/comments

Was: "comments" unordered, and "answers" under each comment unordered
Now: comments newest first, answers oldest first

Both listings had no ORDER BY at all, while the admin listing beside them always had one. The
answers are oldest first because a thread under a question reads forwards; the comments are
newest first, matching "GET /admin/comments".

    GET /admin/audit-logs
    GET /admin/activity-logs

Was: "changes.professors" and "changes.lectures" recorded the id list in the order the
     in-memory Set produced, which can differ between two runs of the same code
Now: sorted

Only the recorded value moved. Whether an assignment counts as changed was already
order-insensitive -- that half was BUG-3's fix; this is the half it did not reach.

Nothing to change if you sort or look up by key. If you render any of these lists in the order
they arrive, they will stop reshuffling.


///// 9.09 (12)


"POST /admin/audit-logs/{id}/revert" refuses an entry whose recorded value cannot be read back,
instead of answering 500.

Was: 500 {"message": "Unexpected backend error", "success": false}
Now: 409 {"message": "The recorded value for '<field>' cannot be read back",
          "success": false, "reason": "ACTION_NOT_REVERTIBLE"}

No new "reason" value -- ACTION_NOT_REVERTIBLE already covers it, so nothing branching on the
five documented reasons has to change.

This cannot be triggered by anything a client sends. It happens when an audit entry names a
value the current code no longer understands: an enum constant that has since been renamed or
removed, or a number that no longer parses. The entry still reports revertible: true, because
revertibility is decided by comparing recorded values as text, so the panel draws the button
and pressing it used to report a backend failure for what is really an unreversible entry.


///// 9.09 (13)


"POST /auth/validate" refuses an address that is not the token owner's.

Was: 200 {"message": "Error, wrong email for auth Token", "success": false}
Now: 401 {"message": "Not logged in", "success": false}

The last route still reporting an authorization failure with 200. Everything else about the
endpoint is unchanged: a matching address still answers
200 {"message": "Correct auth-Token for email: ...", "success": true}.

The message text changed, so anything matching on it has to change. If you were branching on
"success" you now get a 401 for the same case, which is the same answer this endpoint already
gave when the Authorization header was missing entirely.


///// 9.09 (14)


A request body over the upload size limit answers 413 instead of 500.

Was: 500 {"message": "Unexpected backend error", "success": false}
Now: 413 {"message": "Payload too large", "success": false}

No endpoint here accepts a file upload, so this is only reachable by sending a
"multipart/form-data" body to a route that reads JSON. It appears in the production logs, which
is why it is being answered properly rather than left to the catch-all.

A malformed multipart body is unchanged: it still answers 415, because no route in this API can
read that media type. Nothing to change unless you were treating the 500 as a backend outage --
it never was one.


///// 9.09 (15)


"GET /auth/me" is served again. It answers exactly what "GET /admin/auth/me" answers.

Was: 404 {"message": "Not found", "success": false}
Now: 200 {"message": "Authenticated", "success": true, "user": {...}}

The path was removed on the assumption that the panel had already moved to
"GET /admin/auth/me". It had not, and the failure did not look like one from either side: the
panel bare-catches this call after a successful login, turns the 404 into a null identity and
raises "your admin account could not be read", which fails the whole sign-in. The code was
accepted, the token was minted, and nobody could get in.

Requires an admin session, the same as its twin -- an account without one gets 403, not 200.

Nothing to change. If you are already on "GET /admin/auth/me", stay there: this path exists
only until the panel confirms it has migrated, and then it goes again.


///// 9.09 (16)


"POST /answers/report" is served. It answers exactly what "POST /social/answers/report"
answers.

Was: 405 {"message": "Method not allowed", "success": false}, Allow: DELETE, PATCH
Now: 200 {"message": "...", "success": true}

This is not a new feature, it is a route that was never there. The app has always sent
"/answers/report" while the API has only ever served "/social/answers/report", so the request
matched "/answers/{id}" on the moderation controller -- which maps PATCH and DELETE and no
POST -- and was refused. Reporting an answer from the app has never worked. It looked like it
did because the app tells the user the report was submitted before the response arrives.

Requires a session, the same as its "/social" twin.

Nothing to change to get it working; an installed app starts filing reports as soon as this
ships. New work only if you want it on one path: move to "POST /social/answers/report", which
is where every other social route already is, and tell us so this one can go.


///// 9.09 (17)


"GET /account/ratings" returns its ratings in a defined order.

Was: "ratings" in whatever order the database returned them
Now: newest first, ties broken by id

The same defect as F-21, F-27 and 9.09 (11), on the one list those sweeps did not reach. The
lecture inside each entry was already ordered -- "lecture.professors" has been sorted by name
since F-21 -- so the item was ordered inside and unordered outside.

Nothing to change if you sort client-side. If you render the array in the order it arrives, it
will stop reshuffling between two requests for the same account.


///// 10.09 (18)


"GET /users?status=DELETED" lists soft-deleted accounts instead of refusing.

Was: 400, "Deleted users are not listed"
Now: 200, the soft-deleted accounts

Every other call is unchanged, and that is deliberate: an unfiltered listing still excludes
them, and so does a filter for any other status. The panel's account counts come off this
listing, and counting anonymised rows would over-report the size of the platform. "DELETED" is
the only thing that reaches them.

The refusal was never about the filter. Deleted accounts were excluded from every query
unconditionally, so asking for them could only have returned an empty page -- and an empty page
reads as "there are none" rather than "you cannot ask that". The exclusion is conditional now,
so the question is answerable and there is nothing left to refuse.

What comes back is anonymised. A deletion scrubs "username", "kitEmail" and "biography" before
keeping the row, so you get the "id", "joined", "credibilityScore", "warnings" and "reports"
the account had, and "Deleted user <first 8 of the id>" where the identity was. The real
identity is on the matching "USER_DELETED" entry in "GET /audit-logs", whose "target.label" is
written before the scrub. That label is a display string, not two fields: render it, do not
split it.

Read-only. Every mutating route on a deleted account still answers 404.

Nothing to change if you do not ask for them; no existing call moves.


///// 10.09 (19)


"GET /users/{id}" and "GET /users/{id}/warnings" read a soft-deleted account.

Was: 404 for a soft-deleted account, on both
Now: 200 -- the anonymised account, and its warning history

The two move together on purpose. A client that reads an account and its warning history in one
pass and treats a refusal from either as "this account is gone" would have seen no change at all
if only one of them opened; opening one alone would have changed the contract and fixed nothing.

"404" has not gone away and now says one thing instead of two. An id that names no row is still
404 on both routes. An id that names a soft-deleted row -- which exists, anonymised -- is 200.
Before this, 404 answered both "no such account" and "that account was deleted", and there was no
way to tell them apart.

What comes back on the account is anonymised, as in 10.09 (18): "id", "joined",
"credibilityScore", "warnings" and "reports" survive, "Deleted user <first 8 of the id>" stands
where the identity was, and the real identity is on the "USER_DELETED" audit entry. The warning
history is not anonymised -- it is the record of why the account was moderated, and it outlives
the account.

Reading only. Every mutating route on a deleted account still answers 404: the profile edit, the
block and unblock pair, the delete itself, and issuing, editing or deleting a warning. There is
no restore.

Nothing to change if you do not open a deleted account; no existing call moves.


///// 10.09 (20)


Two new "action" values in "GET /audit-logs" and "GET /audit-logs/meta":
"USER_SELF_DELETED" and "USER_SELF_REACTIVATED".

Was: an account that deleted itself through the app left no audit entry at all, and an account
     that came back left none either
Now: both write an entry, in the administrative log

Until now "GET /audit-logs?action=USER_DELETED" answered for admin deletions only. An account
that deleted itself from the app wrote nothing anywhere, so a "DELETED" row in
"GET /users?status=DELETED" with a real username was the only sign it had ever happened, and
there was nothing at all saying when. Both halves of the lifecycle are recorded now.

"USER_SELF_DELETED" -- "PATCH /account/deleteAccount", the app's own deletion.

    {
      "action":"USER_SELF_DELETED",
      "createdAt":"...",
      "actor":{"type":"USER","id":"uuid","name":"ada","email":"ada@student.kit.edu","role":"STUDENT"},
      "target":{"type":"USER","id":"uuid","label":"ada (ada@student.kit.edu)"},
      "changes":{},
      "metadata":{"anonymized":false},
      "revertible":false,
      "revertBlockedReason":"ACTION_NOT_REVERTIBLE",
      "revertedByAuditId":null
    }

"anonymized":false is what separates it from "USER_DELETED": the admin path scrubs the account
in the same transaction and this one does not, so the row keeps its real address.

"USER_SELF_REACTIVATED" -- a deleted account set back to ACTIVE.

    {
      "action":"USER_SELF_REACTIVATED",
      "createdAt":"...",
      "actor":{"type":"USER","id":"uuid","name":"ada","email":"ada@student.kit.edu","role":"STUDENT"},
      "target":{"type":"USER","id":"uuid","label":"ada (ada@student.kit.edu)"},
      "changes":{},
      "metadata":{"trigger":"LOGIN_CODE_REQUEST","callerAuthenticated":false},
      "revertible":false,
      "revertBlockedReason":"ACTION_NOT_REVERTIBLE",
      "revertedByAuditId":null
    }

Read "callerAuthenticated":false literally. The account is revived when a login code is
REQUESTED, not when one is entered, and "POST /auth/request-login" is public -- so the caller
who triggered this proved nothing and need not be the account's owner. The actor is the account
because there is no identified actor to name. That behaviour is an open defect and is not fixed
by this entry; the entry records it.

Both are "ADMINISTRATIVE" scope, so they appear in "GET /audit-logs" next to "USER_DELETED" and
not in "GET /activity-logs", even though the actor is a student. The reader who needs them is an
operator asking where an account went.

Neither is revertible. Both carry "revertible":false and an empty "changes", and both are
outside the revertible set, so "POST /audit-logs/{id}/revert" refuses them with
"ACTION_NOT_REVERTIBLE". There is still no restore.

Nothing to change. The action filter on "GET /audit-logs" is built from
"GET /audit-logs/meta", so the two values appear in it without a client release, and an
unrecognised action renders through the panel's default. If you match on action names, add
these two; if you filter by "USER_DELETED" to find deleted accounts, note that self-deletions
are under "USER_SELF_DELETED" and are NOT anonymised -- their "target.label" and the user row
both carry the real identity.
